@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

@* ConnectionDock: what a Blazor Server screen says when the circuit is in trouble.

   Two elements and two moments. This is the first: the bar the framework reveals when something on
   the circuit failed - a dock at the bottom of the screen carrying an amber mark, one sentence and
   the way out. The second is ReconnectModal, which is the give-up state and is a dialog because by
   then there is nothing left to read the page for.

   The id is the framework's and is not ours to rename: `blazor.web.js` finds this element by it and
   sets `display: block` when a circuit error reaches the browser. Everything else here - the class,
   the mark, the dismissal - is the design's.

   The dock deliberately does not cover the page. A reader whose save failed still needs to see what
   they typed, and a modal over it would be the product taking the screen away at the one moment its
   contents cannot be recovered any other way.

   `role="alert"`, and nothing beside it: the bar appears with no other visible change, so a reader
   who cannot see it is otherwise told nothing at all, and what it announces is that the screen has
   stopped working. One role rather than `status` with `aria-live="assertive"` over the top - status
   already implies polite, so the pair said two different things about the same element. *@

<div id="blazor-error-ui" class="dt-dock" role="alert" data-nosnippet>
    <span class="dt-dock__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="dt-dock__text">@Localizer["CircuitError"]</span>
    <a href="." class="reload dt-dock__action">@Localizer["Reload"]</a>
    @* The framework's own dismissal, kept by its class. The glyph is the icon set's rather than the
       scaffold's ✕ character: NFR-24 has one Close mark, and a second one drawn from a font the
       reader may not have is a mark that is sometimes a box. *@
    <button type="button" class="dismiss dt-dock__dismiss" aria-label="@Localizer["Close"]">
        <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />
    </button>
</div>
